From 35cccd777f910011e8b4dffbeb24a0027bf8db93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jetha Chan <jethachan@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:39:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 302/303] ASoC: codecs: add driver for TI TAS5782M

Add a driver for the Texas Instruments TAS5782M stereo class-D amplifier
with integrated DSP.

The speaker tuning lives in the DSP's coefficient RAM and is written over
I2C after every reset. It is product specific (TI PPC3 output for a given
speaker and enclosure), so it is loaded as firmware rather than built
into the driver: tas5782m_dsp_<name>.bin, a sequence of (register, value)
byte pairs including book and page selects, in the format the tas5805m
driver already uses. A coefficient table in the driver would tie a
generic chip driver to one product and could not be updated without a
kernel change.

The device clocks itself from SCLK (PLL reference set by the
configuration), so the configuration is written on the first unmute,
when the I2S clocks are running, and kept across streams: later streams
only leave standby. The sequence is the one the vendor driver uses:
book 0 / page 0, standby and power down, module and register reset, a
settling delay, then the configuration file with the outputs muted.
Playback is refused if the configuration cannot be written, so the
speaker is never driven without its protection.

Mute is the soft mute in CTRL3 plus the DAC routing; on stop the driver
mutes, waits for the volume ramp and enters standby. Volume is the
digital volume after the DSP (0.5 dB steps), capped at 0 dB so it can
only attenuate after the speaker protection. It starts at -30 dB.

Only 48 kHz is accepted: the DSP configuration is computed for it.

